WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:13.200
It’s the early 14th century and northern
Italy is in a continuous state of battles,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.750
wars and feuds that have persisted for several
centuries now.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:20.910
The source of most of this discord is the
divide between the two Italian factions each

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.640
supporting rivaled political claims by the
Holy Roman Empire and the Pope.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.999
The cities that support the pope are called
Guelfs or the church party and the cities

00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:33.220
that support the Holy Roman Empire are called
Ghibellines or the imperial party.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.950
If you want to find out more about the complicated
political relationship between these two factions

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.800
watch my “””Empire vs The Papacy”””
video.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.140
During this politically divided time the bordering
cities of Bologna and Modena, which where

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:49.090
natural rivals at the best of times, found
themselves at opposing sides.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:54.800
With Bologna being a starch church party supporter
and Modena a starch imperial party supporter.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.990
This meant that the already existing bad blood
between the cities got drastically multiplied

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.890
by the outside influences of the Pope and
the Holy Roman Emperor.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:07.240
Due to all this, the lands of Modena and Bologna
became a constant bickering battleground of

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.150
opposing ideologies.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:15.170
For example in 1249 after the Battle of Fossalta,
in which both Modena and Bologna participated,

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.210
supporting their respective factions, the
victorious Bolognese thought it would be funny

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:24.380
to launch a live donkey over the walls of
Modena and so they did.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.939
Humiliating the Modenese.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:30.770
Continuing from there in 1296 Bologna managed
to captured a few border towns from Modena

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:35.310
and the Pope quickly acknowledged the new
holdings as core Bolognese territory.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.729
But this wasn’t just a one sided conflict.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:43.530
For example the leader of a starch pro empire
leaning Mantua Passerino Bonacolsi also managed

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.420
to become the leader of Modena in 1308.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:51.979
He was known as a loyal agent to the Emperor
Luis the 4th himself and actively waged war

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:56.209
against the cities of Parma and Reggio both
members of the church faction.

00:01:56.209 --> 00:02:00.139
He also lead a bunch of retaliation raids
in to the Bolognese territory.

00:02:00.139 --> 00:02:04.909
These militant actions along with his loyalty
to the emperor, got Passerino excommunicated

00:02:04.909 --> 00:02:10.390
by the Pope, who promised to grant indulgences
befit of a crusader if they harm Passerino

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.270
or his possessions.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.170
Continuing on from there, not much change
for most of the early 14th century, with Modena

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:21.980
and Bologna being continuously at each other's
throats until 1325, when the feud escalated

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.690
in to an all out war.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:28.060
It started off in July of that year, with
Bolognese troops entering Modenese territory

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.730
and laying waste to border towns and fields.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.360
In retaliation, Passerino used the Mantuan
army and entered Bolognese territory besieging

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.000
the fortified stronghold of Monteveglio.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.599
Monteveglio was a very important Bolognese
fortress because it, along with Zappolino

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:45.160
protected the city from any direct seages.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.390
This was due to the fact that if any army
decided to besiege Bologna, they would be

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:53.159
vulnerable to a rear attack directed from
these two fortresses.

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:57.950
Unfortunately for Passerino this also meant
that Monteveglio was a very well fortified

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.549
stronghold due to its strategic importance.

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:04.599
However an interesting thing occurred when
Passerino besieged the fortress.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:09.079
It turned out that the ruler of Monteveglio
was a secret supporter of the imperial party

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:12.650
and switched his allegiance when Passerino
arrived.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.290
Whether Passerino knew about this or not is
unknown but now with Monteveglio in the hands

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:22.409
of the Imperial party, Zappolino was the last
remaining fortress on the road to Bologna

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:25.700
and with that the die was cast for the next
stage.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.920
Passerino didn’t push deeper in to the Bolognese
territory because A. he didn’t have enough

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:38.849
soldiers and B. he technically wasn’t at
war with Bologna, yet.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:43.160
Now it is at this point that most english
sources including Wikipedia, militaryhistorynow,

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:48.970
amusing planet, logan productions, zepherus,
qxire, real life lore, and many more will

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.200
tell you about the amusing way the war started.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.200
Although to be fair to real life lore, he
did delete his video after my mention of it

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.200
in the last video.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.610
The story goes that during this tumultuous
time, some Modenes soldiers managed to sneak

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.470
in to the center of Bologna.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:03.420
Here they found a well with a bucket, which
depending on what source you believe was either

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.390
a simple empty bucket or was filled with loot
from the most recent Bolognese raid and put

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.549
on display in the center of town.

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:15.629
The Modenes soldiers naturally decide to steel
this bucket, either as a symbol to humiliate

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:19.519
the Bolognese, or due to the possible loot
that was stored in the bucket.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:24.130
When the Bolognese found out about this, they
were humiliated, angry, and asked the Modenese

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.550
to return the bucket.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.530
When Modena refused to return the bucket.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.610
Bologna declared war.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.740
Now this would be a very entertaining and
funny story, if it were true.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:34.740
Sadly, it isn’t.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.280
The best way I can put it, is that “The
Idea of Modenes soldiers being able to get

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.930
in to the center of Bologna during this time,
whether trying to infiltrate or not, is just

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:49.110
as ridiculous as the idea of Bologna having
a bucket full of loot on display in the center

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.110
of the city.”

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:54.800
This story of a stolen a bucket being a catalyst
for a declaration of war, was most likely

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.729
a misinterpretation of a 17th century poem
about the war, combined with the fact that

00:04:59.729 --> 00:05:04.169
the Modenes soldiers did steel a bucket during
this war, but that definitely happened at

00:05:04.169 --> 00:05:06.710
the end of the war not at the start.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:11.490
So the actual reason for the declaration of
war by Bologna was a combination of many things,

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.220
including internal politics, the taking of
the Monteveglio by Passerino, the overarching

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.400
Empire vs the Papacy factions and the fact
that Bologna knew they were able to raise

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.210
more men if they declared war right now, then
Modena was.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.560
And so, Bologna declared war.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:31.190
Bologna was able to muster up around 20 to
30 thousand infantry and 2000 to 2500 cavalry

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:32.190
knights.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.280
Modena on the other hand, had to ask for help
from other pro imperial cities since they

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:37.610
didn’t have a big army.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:38.610
In the end thanks to the help from Verona,
Mantua and some Germanic cities, the Modenes

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.349
where able to muster up around 5 to 8 thousand
infantry and roughly the same amount of cavalry

00:05:42.349 --> 00:05:43.900
knights as Bologna.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.449
However even though the Bolognese had a much
larger force the composition of these two

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:49.190
armies was quite different.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.180
The Bolognese infantry although much larger
was ill equipped, inexperienced and over all

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.759
had low moral as a large portion of the soldiers
were forced in to service.

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:02.930
The Modenes infantry on the other hand, was
largely composed of trained retinue of the

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:05.879
various nobleman from the aforementioned cities.

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:09.349
They where highly skilled, experienced and
well equipped.

00:06:09.349 --> 00:06:13.449
When it comes to the cavalry knights, both
sides where roughly on same footing with,

00:06:13.449 --> 00:06:14.449
experience, equipment and training.

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:23.760
The first move of the war was by the Bolognese
whom divided there army in to two.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.080
One part of the army was set to besiege Monteveglio
and the second part was ordered to prevent

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.830
the Modenes army from crossing the Panaro
river.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:34.830
At first this strategy seemed to have proved
effective as the Modenes army was struggling

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.129
to get around the Bolognese defences.

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:39.520
However one night they devised a plan.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.389
They faked an attack on a bridge down the
stream which forced the Bolognese to redirect

00:06:43.389 --> 00:06:45.360
more troops from the mountains.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.379
Right after this the Modenes under the cover
of night quickly moved most of their army

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:53.930
up the stream, leaving only small contingencies
of soldiers along the bank of the river.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:59.449
Then early dawn on November 15th the Modenes
army appeared at the bank of the Panaro river

00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:04.569
near Marano surprising the ill prepared Bolognese
defenders that did not expect to fight most

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:07.699
of the Modenes army this far up in the mountains.

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:11.289
The bolognese defensive river line quickly
shattered, and with the momentum on their

00:07:11.289 --> 00:07:16.479
side the Modenese pushed all the way to Serravalle
where they lit signal fires to let the remaining

00:07:16.479 --> 00:07:21.379
soldiers guarding the river bank know the
main army successfully crossed the river.

00:07:21.379 --> 00:07:25.150
Ones regrouped the Modenes started to march
towards the second most important Bolognese

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:26.780
forretress, Zappolino.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:31.000
Bologna couldn’t afford to lose Zappolino
specially with Monteveglio in Modenes hands

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:36.050
and so they lifted the siege of Monteveglio
and ordered the entire army to regroup at

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:37.050
Zappolino.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.819
With this order, the site of the deciding
battle was set, at the end of the Castalleto

00:07:41.819 --> 00:07:44.940
plateau by the foothill of the Zappolino fortress.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:49.400
By the time both armies could see each other,
it was already late in the day and there’s

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:53.199
no doubt that the soldiers on both sides were
quite tired.

00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:57.840
Usually at this point in most medieval wars
the leaders would signal to build a camp and

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.939
wait till the next day to engage in a battle.

00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:05.150
However, Modenes leaders were convinced the
only way to defeat the superior Bolognese

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:10.199
numbers was to deal a quick surprising and
decisive blow early in the war.

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:14.400
This combined with the Modenes momentum throughout
the day and the fact that the Bolognese army

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.479
was not yet fully organize as more soldiers
were still coming in.

00:08:18.479 --> 00:08:22.280
Meant that Modena had to fight here and now
if they ever hope to win.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:24.509
At least that’s how they saw it.

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:29.259
This resulted in the fact that the battle
itself started around 3:30 to 4 pm giving

00:08:29.259 --> 00:08:34.390
only around 2 hours of usable daylight, after
which if the Modenese didn’t score a decisive

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:39.260
victory they would have to do a long retreat
as they were deep in Bolognese territory.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:42.550
None the less this was a gamble they were
willing to make.

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:48.550
And so with the sun behind them, they lined
up on the plateau, with cavalry behind infantry

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:57.120
and started to march to the beat of the drums
towards now hastily deployed Bolognese line.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.910
As the setting sun glimmered off of the armor
of the bolognese infantry which was a mass

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:08.060
of up to 30 thousands men pact title between
the mountains and the volgolo stream, it became

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:12.350
clear that even though they had the higher
ground and superior numbers they wouldn’t

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.410
be able to use that effectively in such a
tight spot.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:22.070
Suddenly the drums stopped ... and the Modenes
infantry along with the cavalry charged straight

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:29.850
into the Bolognese line.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.880
However what the Bolognese didn’t notice,
was that part of the cavalry diverged from

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:38.250
the main group crossing the stream and going
around a small nearby hill.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:42.380
After which when they crossed the stream again,
they suddenly emerged on the rear side of

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:43.500
the Bolognese army.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.980
The Bolognese were caught by surprise and
even though they tried to use their cavalry

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:51.450
to stop the rear attack, it was too late.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:56.230
Seeing the enemy cavalry on their rear many
Bolognese soldiers thought they got fully

00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:59.660
encircled and started to retreat from the
battle.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:04.540
Breaking the lines and creating a full panic
amongst rest of the troops.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:08.810
However trapped in a valley with the only
escape route now being blocked by the enemy

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:14.040
cavalry meant that most of the soldiers had
to try climbing up the hill to the Zappolino

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.040
fortress.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.920
2000 thousand of them didn’t make it, with
about 500 more dying during the battle itself.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.300
The modenes on the other hand suffered only
around 500 casualties.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:30.180
The battle lasted less than 2 hours and Modena
had it’s decisive victory, with the remaining

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:35.660
Bolognese infantry scattering around the countryside
and the cavalierly being chased back all the

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:39.029
way to Bologna.

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:43.670
After this victory, the following day Modena
besieged and destroyed several fortifications

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:45.840
plus the locks on the Rena river.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.260
When the army finally reached the walls of
Bologna they made camp but didn’t attempt

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.570
to seage the city.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:57.139
Instead they basically through a 3 day long
celebration party and forced the Bolognese

00:10:57.139 --> 00:10:58.139
to watch.

00:10:58.139 --> 00:11:02.800
After the three days and with I imagine a
terrible hangover the Modenes withdrew back

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.700
to their city but not before some of the soldiers
decided it would be funny to steel a bucket

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.830
from a well outside of the city walls.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.240
Why did they do this?

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.269
Well you see it was symbolic.

00:11:13.269 --> 00:11:17.360
The Modenes where skilled at making artesian
wells, which are wells that use the physics

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:21.000
of liquids to basically work as a quote unquote
fountain.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.470
Meaning you don’t need a bucket to get the
water out.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:28.550
Bolognese however didn’t know how to make
these artesian wells and so they had traditional

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:29.899
wells with buckets.

00:11:29.899 --> 00:11:34.180
So the Modenes soldiers stealing the bucket,
where basically saying, “ha look at those

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:37.779
dumb Bolognese, they can’t even get some
water without a bucket”.

00:11:37.779 --> 00:11:42.649
According to tradition, this symbolic bucket
was kept at the Modenes Cathedral after which,

00:11:42.649 --> 00:11:46.741
in modern times it was replaced by a replica
and the orginal was transferred to the City

00:11:46.741 --> 00:11:47.741
hall.

00:11:47.741 --> 00:11:51.730
Now it is debated whether even this original
bucket in the city hall is truly the bucket

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:55.410
from 1325 or just a very old symbolic replica.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:59.500
As far as I know there has been no carbon
dating done on the bucket but at the same

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:04.120
time there hasn’t been any reliable source
that would cast suspicion on the originality

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.340
of the bucket.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:10.180
So far now it is probably safe to say that
the bucket in the city hall is original.

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:15.209
After these events not much more happened
in the war and a few months later in 1326

00:12:15.209 --> 00:12:17.290
a peace treaty was signed.

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:22.740
This peace treaty basically stated that Bologna
will pay heavy war reparations to Modena if

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.639
Modena returned the conquered territory.

00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:31.970
And so the war ended as it started with the
Status Quo unchanged.

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:37.040
If you want to know more about the battle
of Zappolino and the war of the bucket and

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:41.550
can read Italian, I highly recommend the book
The Battle of Zappolino and the kidnapped

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.490
bucket, sadly it’s only in italian, but
it is most likely the best source on the subject

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:47.600
outhere.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.210
For those who don’t follow me on twitter,
I sadly had to skip the April video as I was

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:55.740
too busy but now we should be back on track
with one video a month as always.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:00.699
And not to spoil anything but the June and
July videos are going to be big ones, so stick

00:13:00.699 --> 00:13:02.000
around, for history.

